{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T07:56:12.718Z","data":{"turn":12,"content":"{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Now I have a good understanding of the application. Let me set up my todo list and then analyze the authentication code systematically. Let me also read the rest of the recon deliverable quickly.\\n\\nFrom the recon deliverable, I can identify these key authentication-related issues to investigate:\\n\\n1. **Transport security** - No HTTPS/TLS (HTTP only on port 80 and 3000)\\n2. **Rate limiting** - Need to check if auth endpoints have rate limiting\\n3. **Session cookie flags** - `connect.sid` has `httpOnly: true`, no `sameSite`, `secure` conditional on NODE_ENV\\n4. **JWT after logout** - JWT remains valid for 7 days after logout, no blacklist\\n\\n\\n5. **Auth bypass** - Middleware doesn't return 401 on non-JWT errors, just calls next()\\n6. **Hardcoded secrets** - Both JWT and session secrets are hardcoded defaults that need changing in production\\n7. **Weak bcrypt** - Only 4 rounds for password hashing\\n8. **Password reset tokens** - Reusable within a 24-hour window\\n9. **User enumeration** - Forgot password endpoint leaks user existence by returning 404 for unknowns\\n10. **MailHog exposure** - All emails including password reset links are captured and accessible on port 8025\\n11. **Session fixation and no timeout** - Sessions don't rotate IDs and Redis sessions never expire\\n12. **WebSocket vulnerability** - Default namespace has no authentication, just relies on userId as a query parameter\\n13. **Forged tokens** - The hardcoded JWT secret makes it trivial to create valid tokens\\n\\nNow I'm going to create a todo list and dig into the key source files to understand the full attack surface.\",\"signature\":\"\"}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T07:56:12.718Z"}}
