{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:05:33.597Z","data":{"turn":90,"content":"{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_01WNzn2K758kwUESFMteyk4G\",\"name\":\"Write\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/repos/webvulnbench/deliverables/auth_analysis_deliverable.md\",\"content\":\"# Authentication Analysis Report: HireFlow Freelancer Marketplace\\n\\n## 1. Executive Summary\\n\\n- **Analysis Status:** Complete\\n- **Key Outcome:** Thirteen distinct authentication flaws were identified spanning all methodology categories — transport exposure, rate limiting bypass, session management failures, token management gaps, password reset chain compromise, and WebSocket impersonation. The most critical cluster is the complete password reset account-takeover chain: unauthenticated MailHog web UI (port 8025) exposes live reset tokens, those tokens are reusable and expire in 24 hours (not 1 hour as documented), and the reset URL is forgeable via Host Header Injection. A secondary critical path is JWT session persistence after logout combined with a 7-day token lifetime and no blacklist. Both paths result in full, persistent account takeover with zero interaction beyond initial access.\\n- **Purpose of this Document:** This report provides the strategic context on HireFlow's authentication mechanisms, dominant flaw patterns, and key architectural details necessary to effectively exploit the vulnerabilities listed in the exploitation queue.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Dominant Vulnerability Patterns\\n\\n### Pattern 1: Broken Password Reset Chain (Three Compounding Flaws)\\n\\n- **Description:** Three independent flaws chain into a complete account-takeover via the password reset flow:\\n  1. **MailHog web UI exposure** (`host.docker.internal:8025`, no authentication): all outbound email — including password reset tokens for any user — is readable via a public REST API (`GET /api/v2/messages`). This was confirmed live; reset tokens for all registered users were retrieved in one request.\\n  2. **Reset token not cleared post-use** (`src/auth/auth.service.js:131-138`): `updatePassword()` updates `password_hash` but never nullifies `reset_token` or `reset_token_expires`. The token remains valid for a second (or third) use until it expires.\\n  3. **Reset token window is 24h, not 1h** (`src/auth/auth.controller.js:184`): The email says \\\"expires in 1 hour,\\\" the DB stores a 1-hour expiry timestamp, but the controller checks `tokenAge > 86400000` (86,400,000 ms = 24 hours). The `reset_token_expires` DB field is stored (`auth.service.js:116`) but **never queried** by `findByResetToken()` (`auth.service.js:126-129`).\\n- **Implication:** An attacker who can read MailHog (publicly exposed) can take over any account: trigger reset, read token from MailHog, use token to set attacker-controlled password, then reuse the same token again if needed.\\n- **Representative Findings:** `AUTH-VULN-04`, `AUTH-VULN-05`, `AUTH-VULN-06`.\\n\\n### Pattern 2: Persistent JWT After Logout — No Revocation Mechanism\\n\\n- **Description:** The application issues a JWT with a 7-day TTL at login. Logout (`POST /api/auth/logout`) calls `req.session.destroy()` but takes no action against the JWT. There is no token blacklist, no short expiry, and no rotation. Confirmed live: a token obtained before logout was used successfully against `GET /api/auth/me` after the logout endpoint returned 200.\\n- **Implication:** Stolen, intercepted, or previously issued JWTs function as permanent session tokens for up to 7 days regardless of user logout. Combined with HTTP-only transport (no TLS), the JWT is transmitted in cleartext on every request.\\n- **Representative Findings:** `AUTH-VULN-02`, `AUTH-VULN-01`.\\n\\n### Pattern 3: Absent Rate Limiting on All Authentication Endpoints\\n\\n- **Description:** The `authLimiter` (20 req/15 min, `src/middleware/rateLimiter.js:13-19`) is defined but **never imported or applied** to any route. Only the global `apiLimiter` is imported in `src/index.js:22` and applied at `app.use('/api', apiLimiter)`. In the current deployment (`NODE_ENV=development`), this permits **1000 requests per 15-minute window** — confirmed via `RateLimit-Policy: 1000;w=900` response headers on login and forgot-password. No CAPTCHA or account lockout logic exists anywhere in the codebase.\\n- **Implication:** Login, registration, forgot-password, and reset-password are all open to unrestricted brute force. The 4-round bcrypt (`SALT_ROUNDS = 4`, `auth.service.js:8`) means offline cracking of leaked hashes is extremely fast.\\n- **Representative Findings:** `AUTH-VULN-03`, `AUTH-VULN-09`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Strategic Intelligence for Exploitation\\n\\n- **Authentication Method:** Dual-mode: `express-session` (Redis-backed, `connect.sid` cookie) + JWT Bearer token. The `authenticate` middleware checks session first, falls back to JWT (see `src/middleware/auth.js:6-41`). Both paths load the user from PostgreSQL on every request.\\n- **Session Cookie Details:** Cookie name `connect.sid`. Flags set: `HttpOnly: true`. Flags **missing**: `Secure` (only set if `NODE_ENV === production`; environment is currently `development`), `SameSite` (not configured at all). Observed live: `Set-Cookie: connect.sid=...; Path=/; HttpOnly` — no Secure, no SameSite.\\n- **JWT Details:** Signed with HS256. Secret from `process.env.JWT_SECRET || 'hireflow2024api'` (`src/config/index.js:30`). TTL: 7 days. Payload includes `{ id, email, role, walletBalance }`. No refresh mechanism. No blacklist. Live test confirms the token cannot be validated with the default `hireflow2024api` secret, suggesting `JWT_SECRET` is overridden at runtime — however, the code fallback remains a risk if the env var is unset in any deployment.\\n- **Session Store:** Redis 7 (`host:redis:6379`), **no authentication**, **no TLS**. No session TTL is configured in the session middleware (`src/index.js:61-70`), meaning sessions persist until Redis eviction. Role changes in PostgreSQL do NOT invalidate existing JWTs.\\n- **Password Policy:** Server enforces only `password.length >= 8` (validated in `auth.routes.js:17-19` and double-checked in `auth.controller.js:167`). No complexity requirements, no dictionary check, no MFA.\\n- **Password Hashing:** bcrypt with `SALT_ROUNDS = 4` (`auth.service.js:8`) — deliberately reduced \\\"for faster signups during the beta.\\\" Recommended minimum is 12. This makes offline cracking of leaked hashes trivial.\\n- **WebSocket Identity:** Socket.IO at `ws://host.docker.internal:3000/socket.io`. `userId` read from query parameter (`socket.handshake.query.userId`, `socket.js:18`) with no session/token validation. Any attacker can claim any userId.\\n- **Email Relay:** MailHog at `host.docker.internal:8025` (web UI), `host.docker.internal:1025` (SMTP). No authentication. `GET http://host.docker.internal:8025/api/v2/messages` returns all messages including reset tokens in plain text. Confirmed live with active reset tokens for multiple accounts.\\n- **Key Code Paths:**\\n  - Auth middleware: `src/middleware/auth.js`\\n  - Auth bypass line: `src/middleware/auth.js:36` — `next()` called (without 401) on unexpected non-JWT errors during token processing\\n  - Session setup: `src/index.js:61-70`\\n  - JWT config: `src/config/index.js:28-32`\\n  - Reset token generation: `src/utils/helpers.js:25-29` — `timestamp_base36-sha256(email+timestamp)[:16]`\\n  - Reset token use: `src/auth/auth.controller.js:172-193`\\n  - Password update (no token clear): `src/auth/auth.service.js:131-138`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Vulnerability Details\\n\\n### AUTH-VULN-01: No HTTPS — Credentials and Tokens Transmitted in Cleartext\\n\\n- **Check:** Transport & Caching (Methodology §1)\\n- **Verdict:** VULNERABLE\\n- **Evidence:** Application deployed on `http://host.docker.internal:3000` (port 3000, plain HTTP) and `http://host.docker.internal:80` (Nginx, also plain HTTP). No TLS certificate configured at any layer. The `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=15552000` header is sent over plain HTTP — browsers only honor HSTS headers received over HTTPS, so this header is entirely ineffective. All login credentials, JWTs, and session cookies transit the network in cleartext.\\n- **Missing Defense:** TLS termination and valid HTTPS redirect.\\n\\n### AUTH-VULN-02: JWT Token Remains Valid After Logout (No Blacklist)\\n\\n- **Check:** Token/Session Properties — Expiration & Invalidation (Methodology §4)\\n- **Verdict:** VULNERABLE\\n- **Evidence (Live Confirmed):** `POST /api/auth/logout` destroys `req.session` (`auth.controller.js:112`). The JWT Bearer token issued at login is NOT invalidated. Live test: obtained JWT from `POST /api/auth/login`, called `POST /api/auth/logout`, then immediately called `GET /api/auth/me` with the same JWT — received full user profile (HTTP 200). No blacklist, no short TTL, no revocation endpoint exists.\\n- **File:** `src/auth/auth.controller.js:110-123`, `src/auth/auth.service.js:79-82`\\n- **Missing Defense:** Server-side JWT blacklist (e.g., Redis deny-list keyed by jti), or short JWT TTL (< 15 min) with refresh token rotation.\\n\\n### AUTH-VULN-03: No Auth-Specific Rate Limiting on Login / Register / Forgot-Password / Reset-Password\\n\\n- **Check:** Rate Limiting / CAPTCHA / Monitoring (Methodology §2)\\n- **Verdict:** VULNERABLE\\n- **Evidence (Live Confirmed):** `authLimiter` (20 req/15 min) is defined in `src/middleware/rateLimiter.js:13-19` but is **never imported** in `src/index.js` or `src/auth/auth.routes.js`. Only `apiLimiter` is imported and applied globally. Live headers show `RateLimit-Policy: 1000;w=900` on `POST /api/auth/login` and `POST /api/auth/forgot-password` — 1,000 requests/15 minutes in development mode. No lockout, backoff, or CAPTCHA logic exists in the codebase.\\n- **File:** `src/middleware/rateLimiter.js:13-19`, `src/index.js:22`\\n- **Missing Defense:** Apply `authLimiter` middleware to auth routes, implement account lockout after N failures.\\n\\n### AUTH-VULN-04: Unauthenticated MailHog UI Exposes All Password Reset Tokens\\n\\n- **Check:** Recovery & Logout (Methodology §8)\\n- **Verdict:** VULNERABLE\\n- **Evidence (Live Confirmed):** MailHog web UI on `http://host.docker.internal:8025` is unauthenticated. `GET http://host.docker.internal:8025/api/v2/messages` returns all captured emails including password reset tokens and verification tokens for all registered users. During testing, reset tokens for `audituser@test.com` (tokens: `mnprf0ae-8e2463a34cc7cc69`, `mnpra6hb-abd1d9a03fde2826`) and other users were retrieved in a single unauthenticated API call. Application sends all transactional email to this relay (`src/config/index.js:43-46`, `src/utils/helpers.js:5-8`).\\n- **File:** `src/config/index.js:43-46`, `src/utils/helpers.js:5-8`\\n- **Missing Defense:** Remove MailHog from any internet-accessible deployment; use authenticated SMTP relay for non-development environments; restrict MailHog port via firewall.\\n\\n### AUTH-VULN-05: Password Reset Token Not Invalidated After Use + 24h Effective Window\\n\\n- **Check:** Recovery & Logout (Methodology §8)\\n- **Verdict:** VULNERABLE\\n- **Evidence:** `src/auth/auth.service.js:131-138` — `updatePassword()` updates only `password_hash` and `updated_at`. The `reset_token` and `reset_token_expires` fields are **not cleared**. A subsequent call to `findByResetToken(sameToken)` (`auth.service.js:126-129`) would return the user again, allowing the token to be reused. Additionally, `auth.controller.js:184` checks `tokenAge > 86400000` (24 hours), not 1 hour as claimed in the email text and the DB expiry field (`auth.service.js:116` stores 1-hour expiry). The `reset_token_expires` DB column is never referenced in the query path.\\n- **File:** `src/auth/auth.service.js:126-138`, `src/auth/auth.controller.js:180-184`\\n- **Missing Defense:** Clear `reset_token` and `reset_token_expires` in `updatePassword()`; enforce expiry via DB field in `findByResetToken()`; reduce window to ≤1 hour.\\n\\n### AUTH-VULN-06: Host Header Injection in Password Reset URL\\n\\n- **Check:** Recovery & Logout (Methodology §8)\\n- **Verdict:** VULNERABLE\\n- **Evidence (Live Confirmed):** `src/auth/auth.controller.js:141` — `const resetUrl = \\\\`${req.protocol}://${req.get('host')}/reset-password?token=${resetToken}\\\\`` uses `req.get('host')` directly without validation. Live evidence: MailHog contains an email to `testclient@hireflow.com` with reset URL `http://evil.com/reset-password?token=mnpndvri-e87ef5934f152f34` — proving that a request with `Host: evil.com` header generates a password reset email pointing to the attacker's domain.\\n- **File:** `src/auth/auth.controller.js:141`\\n- **Missing Defense:** Use a hardcoded base URL from server configuration, never from the `Host` request header.\\n\\n### AUTH-VULN-07: User Enumeration via Forgot-Password Endpoint\\n\\n- **Check:** Login/Signup Responses (Methodology §7)\\n- **Verdict:** VULNERABLE\\n- **Evidence (Live Confirmed):** `POST /api/auth/forgot-password` returns HTTP 404 with body `{\\\"message\\\":\\\"No account found with that email address\\\"}` for unknown emails, vs HTTP 200 `{\\\"message\\\":\\\"Password reset link sent to your email\\\"}` for registered emails. Code: `auth.controller.js:134-136`. Confirmed live.\\n- **File:** `src/auth/auth.controller.js:134-136`\\n- **Missing Defense:** Return the same HTTP status (200) and same generic message regardless of whether the email is registered.\\n\\n### AUTH-VULN-08: Session Cookie Missing Secure Flag and SameSite Attribute\\n\\n- **Check:** Session Management (Methodology §3)\\n- **Verdict:** VULNERABLE\\n- **Evidence (Live Confirmed):** Session configuration at `src/index.js:66-69`: `secure: config.env === 'production'` — with `NODE_ENV=development` (confirmed via `/api/debug/info`), the `Secure` flag is absent. No `sameSite` option is configured at all. Live: `Set-Cookie: connect.sid=...; Path=/; HttpOnly` — missing both `Secure` and `SameSite`. Combined with no HTTPS, cookies transmit in cleartext. Missing SameSite enables cross-site request forgery using the session cookie.\\n- **File:** `src/index.js:61-70`\\n- **Missing Defense:** Set `secure: true` unconditionally; add `sameSite: 'Lax'` or `'Strict'`.\\n\\n### AUTH-VULN-09: Weak bcrypt Work Factor (4 Rounds)\\n\\n- **Check:** Password & Account Policy (Methodology §6)\\n- **Verdict:** VULNERABLE\\n- **Evidence:** `src/auth/auth.service.js:8` — `const SALT_ROUNDS = 4;` with inline comment \\\"keeping rounds low for faster signups during the beta.\\\" bcrypt at 4 rounds is approximately 256x faster than the OWASP-recommended 12 rounds. If PostgreSQL credentials leak (port 5432 is directly exposed, no auth separation), hashes can be cracked in seconds on modern GPU hardware.\\n- **File:** `src/auth/auth.service.js:8`\\n- **Missing Defense:** Increase to `SALT_ROUNDS = 12` minimum; rehash on next login.\\n\\n### AUTH-VULN-10: WebSocket User Impersonation (No Authentication)\\n\\n- **Check:** Session Management / Token Properties (Methodology §3, §4)\\n- **Verdict:** VULNERABLE\\n- **Evidence:** `src/config/socket.js:18` — `const userId = socket.handshake.query.userId;` — userId is taken directly from the WebSocket query string with no session or token verification. Any attacker can connect to `ws://host.docker.internal:3000/socket.io?userId=<victim_id>` and be treated as that user. Events `join:conversation`, `leave:conversation`, and `typing` (which also takes userId from client payload, `socket.js:35`) are all unauthenticated.\\n- **File:** `src/config/socket.js:18`, `src/config/socket.js:35`\\n- **Missing Defense:** Validate userId against `req.session` or a verified JWT during Socket.IO handshake; reject connections with no valid credential.\\n\\n### AUTH-VULN-11: Auth Middleware Bypass on Unexpected Database/Runtime Error\\n\\n- **Check:** Session Management — correct auth state enforcement (Methodology §3)\\n- **Verdict:** VULNERABLE (Medium confidence — hard to trigger externally)\\n- **Evidence:** `src/middleware/auth.js:31-37`: when JWT verification succeeds but the subsequent DB query (`db('users').where({ id: decoded.id }).first()`) throws an unexpected error (non-`JsonWebTokenError`, non-`TokenExpiredError`), the catch block calls `return next()` **without** returning a 401. `req.user` remains undefined, but the request proceeds to the next handler. Any route that does not defensively check `req.user` before use would then fail open. Real-world triggers: transient database connectivity loss, query timeout, unexpected DB exception.\\n- **File:** `src/middleware/auth.js:35-36`\\n- **Missing Defense:** Replace `return next()` with `return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Authentication required' })` in the unexpected-error branch.\\n\\n### AUTH-VULN-12: No Session ID Regeneration After Login (Session Fixation)\\n\\n- **Check:** Session Fixation (Methodology §5)\\n- **Verdict:** VULNERABLE (Low-Medium confidence — mitigated by `saveUninitialized: false`)\\n- **Evidence:** `src/auth/auth.controller.js:94-96` — login sets `req.session.userId = user.id` without calling `req.session.regenerate()`. If an attacker can pre-plant a known session ID (e.g., by observing network traffic or manipulating a shared browser), that same session ID is elevated to authenticated status after login. The `saveUninitialized: false` flag reduces direct risk by not issuing a session ID before login, but the absence of regeneration is still a code-level flaw.\\n- **File:** `src/auth/auth.controller.js:94-96`\\n- **Missing Defense:** Call `req.session.regenerate(callback)` before setting `req.session.userId`.\\n\\n### AUTH-VULN-13: Hardcoded JWT Default Secret in Source Code\\n\\n- **Check:** Token Properties (Methodology §4)\\n- **Verdict:** VULNERABLE (Medium confidence — live instance may override with env var)\\n- **Evidence:** `src/config/index.js:30` — `secret: process.env.JWT_SECRET || 'hireflow2024api'`. No `.env` file exists on disk; the `env_file: - .env` reference in `docker-compose.yml` points to a non-existent file. If `JWT_SECRET` is not set in the runtime environment, the application falls back to `'hireflow2024api'` — a known, public default. Live JWT signature verification with this default was inconclusive (signature mismatch), suggesting the env var may be set at container runtime. However, the code fallback means any deployment without explicit `JWT_SECRET` configuration is immediately exploitable for token forgery with administrative privilege escalation (`role: \\\"superadmin\\\"` in forged payload).\\n- **File:** `src/config/index.js:30`\\n- **Missing Defense:** Remove fallback default; require `JWT_SECRET` from environment (crash on startup if unset).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Secure by Design: Validated Components\\n\\nThese components were analyzed and found to have adequate defenses. They are low-priority for further testing.\\n\\n| Component/Flow | Endpoint/File Location | Defense Mechanism Implemented | Verdict |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Password hashing algorithm | `src/auth/auth.service.js:60-62` | Uses `bcrypt.compare()` for constant-time comparison — not vulnerable to timing attacks | SAFE (work factor issue is separate finding AUTH-VULN-09) |\\n| Role coercion at registration | `src/auth/auth.controller.js:28` | Self-registration limited to `client`/`freelancer` only; no path to `admin`/`moderator`/`superadmin` via self-service | SAFE |\\n| RBAC middleware structure | `src/middleware/rbac.js` | `requireRole()`, `requireAdmin()`, `requireModerator()` check `req.user.role` consistently; `superadmin` bypass is intentional hierarchy | SAFE |\\n| Login error messages | `src/auth/auth.controller.js:78`, `87` | Both user-not-found and wrong-password return identical message `\\\"Invalid email or password\\\"` — no per-field enumeration on login | SAFE |\\n| Email verification token entropy | `src/utils/helpers.js:20-22` | `crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('hex')` — 256 bits of cryptographic randomness | SAFE |\\n| Logout session destruction | `src/auth/auth.controller.js:112` | `req.session.destroy()` correctly invalidates the server-side Redis session entry | SAFE (JWT non-invalidation is separate finding AUTH-VULN-02) |\\n| Admin-only role escalation | `src/admin/admin.controller.js:33-60` | Role upgrades require `requireAdmin` middleware; no self-service escalation path | SAFE |\\n\"},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:05:33.597Z"}}
