{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:07:22.415Z","data":{"turn":93,"content":"{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_01DjQhrESmj4e7gopJs7DPnS\",\"name\":\"Bash\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"save-deliverable --type AUTH_QUEUE --content '{\\n  \\\"vulnerabilities\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-01\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Transport_Exposure\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/login, POST /api/auth/register, GET /api/auth/me, ALL auth endpoints\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/index.js (no TLS config); nginx/default.conf (HTTP only port 80); Strict-Transport-Security header sent over plain HTTP at src/index.js:50-53 (helmet config)\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"No HTTPS/TLS at any layer (Nginx port 80, Express port 3000 both HTTP). The HSTS header (max-age=15552000) is transmitted over plain HTTP and is therefore completely ineffective — browsers only enforce HSTS received over HTTPS. All credentials, JWTs, and session cookies traverse the network in cleartext.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker with network interception capability (e.g., same LAN, rogue AP, or malicious ISP node) can capture plaintext login credentials (email + password) and session cookies or JWT tokens from in-flight HTTP requests, then replay them to authenticate as the victim user.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"session_hijacking\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Both port 80 (Nginx) and port 3000 (Express direct) are HTTP-only. The session cookie lacks Secure flag (see AUTH-VULN-08), making it transmittable over HTTP. Every authenticated API call leaks the JWT in the Authorization header or the connect.sid in Cookie header in cleartext.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-02\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Token_Management_Issue\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/logout\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/auth/auth.controller.js:110-123 (logout destroys session only); src/auth/auth.service.js:79-82 (JWT signed with 7d TTL, no jti or revocation)\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"Logout only destroys the server-side Redis session via req.session.destroy(). The JWT Bearer token issued at login continues to be accepted by the authenticate middleware (src/middleware/auth.js:21-37) for its full 7-day lifetime. No JWT blacklist, deny-list, or jti tracking exists anywhere in the codebase.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker who obtains a victim JWT (via network interception, XSS, or shoulder-surfing) can continue to authenticate as the victim indefinitely (up to 7 days) even after the victim explicitly logs out, bypassing the logout security control entirely.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"token_replay\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"CONFIRMED LIVE: JWT token used against GET /api/auth/me returned HTTP 200 with full user profile after POST /api/auth/logout returned success. JWT payload includes { id, email, role, walletBalance } — all seven-day-valid credentials for any authenticated API call.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-03\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Abuse_Defenses_Missing\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/login, POST /api/auth/register, POST /api/auth/forgot-password, POST /api/auth/reset-password\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/middleware/rateLimiter.js:13-19 (authLimiter defined but unused); src/index.js:22 (only apiLimiter imported); src/auth/auth.routes.js (no rate limit middleware applied)\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"The authLimiter (20 req/15 min) is defined but never imported or applied. All four authentication endpoints are protected only by the global apiLimiter which allows 1,000 requests per 15-minute window in development mode (NODE_ENV=development confirmed). No account lockout, exponential backoff, or CAPTCHA exists anywhere in the codebase.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker can submit up to 1,000 login attempts per 15 minutes without triggering any throttle or lockout, enabling brute-force of user passwords or credential stuffing with leaked password lists. Combined with weak bcrypt (4 rounds, AUTH-VULN-09), password cracking is extremely fast both online and offline.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"brute_force_login\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"CONFIRMED LIVE via RateLimit-Policy: 1000;w=900 response header on POST /api/auth/login and POST /api/auth/forgot-password. Environment is development (per /api/debug/info) so production limit of 200/15min also applies to login — still insufficient. No CAPTCHA, no lockout. Try common passwords against enumerated users (user enumeration available via AUTH-VULN-07).\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-04\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Reset_Recovery_Flaw\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/forgot-password (triggers email); GET http://host.docker.internal:8025/api/v2/messages (MailHog API)\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/config/index.js:43-46 (SMTP config pointing to MailHog); src/utils/helpers.js:5-8 (nodemailer transporter with ignoreTLS: true); docker-compose.yml (mailhog service with ports 1025:1025, 8025:8025 exposed, no auth)\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"MailHog is a development email relay that captures all outbound email in memory. Its web UI and REST API (port 8025) are exposed on all host interfaces with zero authentication. All password reset tokens and email verification tokens for all users are readable via a single unauthenticated HTTP GET.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker can take over any user account by: (1) calling POST /api/auth/forgot-password with the target email, (2) immediately fetching GET http://host.docker.internal:8025/api/v2/messages to read the reset token, (3) calling POST /api/auth/reset-password with the stolen token and an attacker-controlled password — achieving full account takeover in three requests.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"reset_token_guessing\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"CONFIRMED LIVE: Retrieved active reset tokens for multiple users including audituser@test.com (tokens: mnprf0ae-8e2463a34cc7cc69, mnpra6hb-abd1d9a03fde2826) and testclient@hireflow.com (mnpng3cy-48091580948362f9, mnpndvri-e87ef5934f152f34) via single unauthenticated request to MailHog API. Token format: {timestamp_base36}-{sha256(email+timestamp)[:16]}. Chained with AUTH-VULN-05 (reusable tokens) for repeat access.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-05\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Reset_Recovery_Flaw\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/reset-password\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/auth/auth.service.js:131-138 (updatePassword does not clear reset_token); src/auth/auth.controller.js:180-184 (checks tokenAge > 86400000 = 24h, not 1h); src/auth/auth.service.js:126-129 (findByResetToken does not filter by reset_token_expires)\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"Two compounding flaws: (1) updatePassword() sets only password_hash — never nullifies reset_token or reset_token_expires columns, allowing the same token to be reused for unlimited password resets within the expiry window. (2) The effective expiry window is 24 hours (86,400,000 ms checked in controller), not 1 hour as stated in the email and stored in reset_token_expires (which is never queried).\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker who obtains a reset token (via AUTH-VULN-04 MailHog or AUTH-VULN-06 Host Header Injection) can use it to reset the password, then use the same token again to reset it a second time — locking the legitimate user out permanently. The 24-hour window also gives the attacker ample time to act without urgency.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"reset_token_guessing\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Code confirmed: updatePassword() at auth.service.js:131-138 has no reset_token: null in the update object. findByResetToken() at auth.service.js:126-129 uses only .where({ reset_token: token }) with no expiry check. The DB column reset_token_expires is write-only. Controller at auth.controller.js:184 uses 86400000 (24h) despite the email and service comment saying 1 hour.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-06\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Reset_Recovery_Flaw\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/forgot-password\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/auth/auth.controller.js:141\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"The password reset URL is constructed using req.get('host') without validation: `${req.protocol}://${req.get('host')}/reset-password?token=${resetToken}`. An attacker who controls the Host request header can cause the application to send a password reset email containing a link pointing to an attacker-controlled domain, where the token is surrendered when the victim clicks the link.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker sends POST /api/auth/forgot-password with a victim email and a spoofed Host header (e.g., Host: attacker.com). The application sends the victim a password reset email with a link to http://attacker.com/reset-password?token=<valid_token>. When the victim clicks the link, the token is transmitted to the attacker's server, enabling account takeover.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"reset_token_guessing\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"CONFIRMED LIVE: MailHog contains email to testclient@hireflow.com with reset URL http://evil.com/reset-password?token=mnpndvri-e87ef5934f152f34 — proving Host header injection works. Attack does not require MITM — only a forged HTTP request header. Effective even when MailHog is not accessible externally.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-07\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Login_Flow_Logic\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/forgot-password\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/auth/auth.controller.js:134-136\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/forgot-password returns HTTP 404 with message 'No account found with that email address' for unregistered emails, vs HTTP 200 with 'Password reset link sent to your email' for registered emails. This allows an attacker to confirm whether any email address has a registered account.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker can enumerate all registered user email addresses by submitting a list of candidate emails to POST /api/auth/forgot-password and observing the HTTP status code differential (404 = not registered, 200 = registered). This enables targeted brute-force (AUTH-VULN-03) and social engineering attacks against confirmed accounts.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"account_enumeration\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"CONFIRMED LIVE: POST with audituser@test.com returns 200 + 'Password reset link sent to your email'. POST with nonexistent99999@test.com returns 404 + 'No account found with that email address'. No rate limiting on this endpoint (AUTH-VULN-03). Combined with AUTH-VULN-04 and AUTH-VULN-03 forms a complete reconnaissance-to-takeover chain.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-08\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Session_Management_Flaw\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"ALL authenticated endpoints using Cookie: connect.sid\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/index.js:61-70 (session config: secure conditional on production, no sameSite)\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"Session cookie missing two security attributes: (1) Secure flag is absent because config.env !== 'production' (environment is 'development') — the cookie is transmitted over plaintext HTTP. (2) SameSite attribute is not configured at all — the cookie will be sent on cross-site requests, enabling CSRF-based session abuse. CORS policy reflects any origin with credentials (src/index.js:46-49), amplifying the SameSite omission.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker on the same network can intercept the plaintext session cookie from HTTP traffic and replay it to authenticate as the victim. Separately, the missing SameSite attribute allows cross-origin requests to carry the session cookie, enabling CSRF attacks against state-changing endpoints.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"session_hijacking\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"CONFIRMED LIVE: Login response Set-Cookie header: 'connect.sid=...; Path=/; HttpOnly' — no Secure, no SameSite. CORS: 'origin: true, credentials: true' at src/index.js:46-49 reflects any origin. The Secure flag absence is particularly impactful given the HTTP-only transport (AUTH-VULN-01).\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-09\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Abuse_Defenses_Missing\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/register, POST /api/auth/login (password verification path)\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/auth/auth.service.js:8 (SALT_ROUNDS = 4)\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"bcrypt work factor is set to 4 rounds with an inline comment acknowledging this is intentionally low 'for faster signups during the beta'. OWASP recommends a minimum of 12 rounds. At 4 rounds, bcrypt is approximately 256x faster than at 12 rounds, making offline cracking of leaked PostgreSQL hashes trivial with commodity GPU hardware.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"If password hashes are obtained (e.g., via SQL injection on the exposed /api/users?search= or /api/admin/users?search= endpoints, or direct PostgreSQL access on exposed port 5432), an attacker can crack the 4-round bcrypt hashes in minutes using tools such as hashcat, enabling full credential recovery for all users.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"credential_stuffing\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Code confirmed at src/auth/auth.service.js:8. This finding is a force-multiplier for other vulnerabilities (SQLi hash exfiltration + rapid offline crack = full account list compromise). No minimum password complexity beyond 8-character length, increasing the proportion of crackable passwords.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-10\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Session_Management_Flaw\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"ws://host.docker.internal:3000/socket.io?userId=<any>\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/config/socket.js:18 (userId from query param without verification); src/config/socket.js:35 (typing event userId from client payload)\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"Socket.IO server reads userId from the client-supplied query parameter (socket.handshake.query.userId) with no session cookie or JWT verification during the handshake. Any WebSocket client can claim any userId. The typing event further takes userId from the client event payload rather than any server-side identity, making it doubly spoofable.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker can connect to the WebSocket endpoint claiming to be any user (e.g., ?userId=<victim_id>), receive real-time events destined for that user, join any conversation room (join:conversation event has no membership check), and inject typing presence indicators impersonating any user — all without valid credentials.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"session_hijacking\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Code at src/config/socket.js:18-23: userId is stored in onlineUsers map and broadcast to all clients (io.emit('user:online')) with no authentication. No session or token validation at handshake. CORS for Socket.IO is 'origin: *' (src/config/socket.js:8-11). The messaging namespace (/messaging) is dead code per recon but the default namespace is fully active and unauthenticated.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-11\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Authentication_Bypass\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"ALL endpoints protected by authenticate middleware\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/middleware/auth.js:31-36\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"In the JWT verification try/catch block, only JsonWebTokenError and TokenExpiredError are handled with a 401 response. Any other exception (e.g., database connection failure, query timeout, unexpected runtime error during db('users').where().first()) falls through to 'return next()' at line 36 — advancing the request pipeline with req.user === undefined. Routes that do not defensively null-check req.user before use could process the request as if authenticated.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker who can trigger a transient database error (e.g., by saturating PostgreSQL connections or exploiting a timing window during DB restart) while sending a request with any Bearer token could cause the authenticate middleware to call next() without setting req.user, potentially bypassing authentication on susceptible route handlers.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"session_hijacking\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"Medium\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"The flaw is deterministic in code but requires an external trigger (DB error) to manifest. Practical exploitability depends on the attacker's ability to cause DB-level errors. However, under sustained DoS on the DB layer, any request with a syntactically valid Bearer token (even random bytes that don't parse as a JWT error) would pass through. Medium confidence due to required precondition.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-12\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Token_Management_Issue\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"ALL authenticated endpoints using JWT Bearer\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/config/index.js:30\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"JWT secret falls back to hardcoded value 'hireflow2024api' when JWT_SECRET environment variable is not set. No .env file exists on disk (confirmed via filesystem check). Code: `secret: process.env.JWT_SECRET || 'hireflow2024api'`. If the running container is not supplied the JWT_SECRET env var, the default is used and any attacker with knowledge of this public default can forge arbitrary JWTs with any role (including superadmin).\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"If the JWT_SECRET env var is unset, an attacker can forge a JWT signed with 'hireflow2024api' claiming role: 'superadmin' and arbitrary user ID, gaining unrestricted administrative access to all API endpoints including user role management (PUT /api/admin/users/:id), platform settings (PUT /api/admin/settings), and audit logs (GET /api/admin/audit-log).\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"token_replay\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"Medium\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Live JWT signature verification with 'hireflow2024api' did not match the observed token, suggesting JWT_SECRET env var may be set at container runtime via a mechanism not visible on disk. However, the code-level fallback is confirmed. Test by forging a HS256 JWT with secret 'hireflow2024api' and { id: <valid_user_id>, role: 'superadmin', exp: now+86400 } and sending to GET /api/admin/dashboard. No .env file at /repos/webvulnbench/.env.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-13\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Login_Flow_Logic\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/login\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/auth/auth.controller.js:94-96\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"After successful password verification, the login handler sets req.session.userId = user.id without calling req.session.regenerate(). This means if a session already exists (with some session ID), that same session ID is elevated to an authenticated session. The saveUninitialized: false setting reduces practical risk by not issuing pre-login session IDs in most scenarios, but the missing regenerate() call is a structural flaw.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker who can pre-plant a known session ID in the victim's browser (e.g., via network interception on HTTP, or via a shared device) and then the victim logs in, the attacker retains access to the now-authenticated session using the known session ID — without knowing the victim's credentials.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"session_fixation\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"Low\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Practical risk is reduced by saveUninitialized: false (no session issued before login under normal flow). However, if any middleware or route handler modifies the session before login (initializing it), the fixation becomes exploitable. Low confidence for exploitation but code fix is trivial and mandatory per OWASP.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}'\",\"description\":\"Save auth exploitation queue with all 13 vulnerabilities\"},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:07:22.415Z"}}
{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:08:46.089Z","data":{"turn":94,"content":"{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_01UGL3A6mmrMPuvNaZV9nsWT\",\"name\":\"Write\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/repos/webvulnbench/deliverables/auth_exploitation_queue.json\",\"content\":\"{\\n  \\\"vulnerabilities\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-01\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Transport_Exposure\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/login\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/index.js:50-53 (helmet config, no TLS); nginx/default.conf (HTTP port 80 only); HSTS header sent over plain HTTP is ineffective\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"No HTTPS/TLS at any layer. Nginx serves on port 80 (HTTP only) and Express is directly accessible on port 3000 (HTTP). The Strict-Transport-Security header transmitted over plain HTTP is meaningless. All credentials, JWTs, and session cookies traverse the network in cleartext.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker with network interception capability can capture plaintext login credentials and session tokens from in-flight HTTP requests, then replay them to authenticate as the victim user.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"session_hijacking\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Both port 80 and port 3000 are HTTP-only. Session cookie lacks Secure flag (AUTH-VULN-08). Every authenticated API call leaks JWT in Authorization header or connect.sid in Cookie header in cleartext.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-02\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Token_Management_Issue\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/logout\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/auth/auth.controller.js:110-123 (logout destroys session only); src/auth/auth.service.js:79-82 (JWT signed with 7d TTL, no jti or revocation)\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"Logout only destroys the server-side Redis session. The JWT Bearer token remains accepted by authenticate middleware for its full 7-day lifetime. No JWT blacklist, deny-list, or jti tracking exists anywhere in the codebase.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker who obtains a victim JWT can continue to authenticate as the victim for up to 7 days even after the victim explicitly logs out, bypassing the logout security control entirely.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"token_replay\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"CONFIRMED LIVE: JWT used against GET /api/auth/me returned HTTP 200 with full user profile immediately after POST /api/auth/logout returned success. JWT payload includes id, email, role, walletBalance — all valid for 7 days.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-03\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Abuse_Defenses_Missing\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/login\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/middleware/rateLimiter.js:13-19 (authLimiter defined but unused); src/index.js:22 (only apiLimiter imported); src/auth/auth.routes.js (no rate limit middleware applied to any route)\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"The authLimiter (20 req/15 min) is defined but never imported or applied. All auth endpoints are subject only to the global apiLimiter which allows 1000 requests per 15 minutes in development mode. No account lockout, backoff, or CAPTCHA exists anywhere.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker can submit up to 1000 login attempts per 15 minutes without triggering any throttle or lockout, enabling brute-force of user passwords or credential stuffing with leaked password lists.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"brute_force_login\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"CONFIRMED LIVE via RateLimit-Policy: 1000;w=900 header on POST /api/auth/login and POST /api/auth/forgot-password. Applies equally to /register and /reset-password. NODE_ENV=development confirmed via /api/debug/info. No CAPTCHA, no lockout. Use enumerated emails from AUTH-VULN-07 as targets.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-04\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Reset_Recovery_Flaw\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/forgot-password\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/config/index.js:43-46 (SMTP config pointing to MailHog); src/utils/helpers.js:5-8 (nodemailer transporter); docker-compose.yml mailhog service (ports 8025:8025 exposed, no auth)\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"MailHog development email relay (port 8025) is exposed on all host interfaces with zero authentication. All password reset tokens and email verification tokens for all users are readable via a single unauthenticated HTTP GET to the MailHog REST API.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker can take over any user account by triggering POST /api/auth/forgot-password with the target email, then fetching GET http://host.docker.internal:8025/api/v2/messages to read the reset token, then calling POST /api/auth/reset-password to set an attacker-controlled password — achieving full account takeover in three requests.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"reset_token_guessing\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"CONFIRMED LIVE: Retrieved active reset tokens for multiple users via single unauthenticated request to MailHog API. Tokens confirmed for audituser@test.com and testclient@hireflow.com. Chained with AUTH-VULN-05 (reusable tokens with 24h window) for persistent access.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-05\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Reset_Recovery_Flaw\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/reset-password\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/auth/auth.service.js:131-138 (updatePassword does not clear reset_token); src/auth/auth.controller.js:180-184 (checks tokenAge > 86400000 = 24h not 1h); src/auth/auth.service.js:126-129 (findByResetToken does not filter by reset_token_expires)\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"Two compounding flaws: updatePassword() never nullifies reset_token or reset_token_expires columns, allowing the same token to be reused for unlimited password resets within the expiry window. The effective expiry window is 24 hours (86400000 ms in controller) not 1 hour as claimed in the email. The reset_token_expires DB column is stored but never queried.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker who obtains a reset token can use it to reset the password, then use the same token again to reset it a second time, permanently locking the legitimate user out. The 24-hour window gives ample time for multi-stage attack.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"reset_token_guessing\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Code confirmed: updatePassword() at auth.service.js:131-138 has no reset_token nullification. findByResetToken() uses only .where({ reset_token: token }) with no expiry check. Controller at auth.controller.js:184 uses 86400000 (24h). DB stores reset_token_expires but it is write-only.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-06\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Reset_Recovery_Flaw\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/forgot-password\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/auth/auth.controller.js:141\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"The password reset URL is constructed using req.get('host') without validation. An attacker who controls the Host request header can cause the application to generate a password reset email containing a link pointing to an attacker-controlled domain.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker sends POST /api/auth/forgot-password with a victim email and a spoofed Host header (e.g., Host: attacker.com). The application sends the victim a reset email with a link to http://attacker.com/reset-password?token=valid_token. When the victim clicks the link, the token is transmitted to the attacker's server, enabling account takeover.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"reset_token_guessing\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"CONFIRMED LIVE: MailHog contains email to testclient@hireflow.com with reset URL http://evil.com/reset-password?token=mnpndvri-e87ef5934f152f34 proving Host header injection works. Attack does not require MITM. Only requires a forged HTTP Host header in the forgot-password request.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-07\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Login_Flow_Logic\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/forgot-password\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/auth/auth.controller.js:134-136\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"Forgot-password endpoint returns HTTP 404 with message 'No account found with that email address' for unregistered emails vs HTTP 200 with 'Password reset link sent to your email' for registered emails. The HTTP status code difference allows email enumeration.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker can confirm whether any email address has a registered account, enabling targeted brute-force against confirmed users and reducing the search space for credential stuffing attacks.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"account_enumeration\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"CONFIRMED LIVE: audituser@test.com returns 200, nonexistent99999@test.com returns 404. No rate limiting on this endpoint (AUTH-VULN-03). Enables efficient targeted attack: enumerate accounts first, then brute-force or use reset-chain takeover via AUTH-VULN-04.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-08\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Session_Management_Flaw\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"ALL authenticated endpoints using Cookie: connect.sid\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/index.js:61-70 (session config: secure conditional on production, no sameSite configured)\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"Session cookie missing two security attributes: Secure flag absent because NODE_ENV is development; SameSite attribute not configured at all. The CORS policy reflects any origin with credentials (origin: true, credentials: true at src/index.js:46-49), amplifying the missing SameSite.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker on the same network can intercept the plaintext session cookie from HTTP traffic and replay it to authenticate as the victim. The missing SameSite attribute allows cross-origin requests to carry the session cookie, enabling CSRF-based session abuse.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"session_hijacking\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"CONFIRMED LIVE: Login Set-Cookie header: 'connect.sid=...; Path=/; HttpOnly' with no Secure and no SameSite. HTTP-only transport (AUTH-VULN-01) makes Secure flag absence directly exploitable. Wildcard CORS with credentials enabled further expands attack surface.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-09\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Abuse_Defenses_Missing\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/register\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/auth/auth.service.js:8\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"bcrypt work factor set to 4 rounds (SALT_ROUNDS = 4) with comment acknowledging this is intentionally low for beta performance. OWASP recommends minimum 12 rounds. At 4 rounds bcrypt is 256x faster than at 12 rounds, making offline cracking of leaked hashes trivial with GPU hardware.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"If password hashes are obtained via SQL injection on exposed endpoints or direct PostgreSQL access on exposed port 5432, an attacker can crack the 4-round bcrypt hashes in minutes using hashcat, enabling full credential recovery for all users.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"credential_stuffing\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Code confirmed at src/auth/auth.service.js:8. Force-multiplier for SQLi findings — hash exfiltration plus rapid offline crack equals full account list compromise. No password complexity requirements beyond 8-character minimum increases proportion of crackable passwords.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-10\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Session_Management_Flaw\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"ws://host.docker.internal:3000/socket.io\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/config/socket.js:18 (userId from query param without verification); src/config/socket.js:35 (typing event userId from client payload)\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"Socket.IO server reads userId from client-supplied query parameter with no session cookie or JWT verification during the handshake. Any WebSocket client can claim any userId. The typing event further takes userId from the client event payload making it doubly spoofable.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker can connect to the WebSocket endpoint claiming to be any user, receive real-time events destined for that user, join any conversation room, and inject typing presence indicators impersonating any user — all without valid credentials.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"session_hijacking\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"High\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Code at src/config/socket.js:18-23: userId stored in onlineUsers map and broadcast to all clients without authentication. CORS for Socket.IO is origin: '*'. Join conversation has no membership check. Default namespace is fully active and unauthenticated per recon.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-11\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Authentication_Bypass\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"ALL endpoints protected by authenticate middleware\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/middleware/auth.js:35-36\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"In the JWT verification catch block, only JsonWebTokenError and TokenExpiredError are handled with a 401 response. Any other exception during the DB lookup after JWT verification falls through to return next() at line 36 with req.user undefined, potentially bypassing authentication on route handlers that do not null-check req.user.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker who can trigger a transient database error while sending a request with any syntactically valid Bearer token string could cause the authenticate middleware to call next() without setting req.user, bypassing authentication on susceptible route handlers.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"session_hijacking\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"Medium\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Flaw is deterministic in code but requires external trigger (DB error). Practical exploitability depends on ability to cause DB-level errors. Under sustained pressure on PostgreSQL connection pool, requests with any Bearer token string may pass through unauthenticated.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-12\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Token_Management_Issue\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"ALL authenticated endpoints using JWT Bearer\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/config/index.js:30\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"JWT secret falls back to hardcoded value 'hireflow2024api' when JWT_SECRET environment variable is not set. No .env file exists on disk. If the runtime environment variable is unset, any attacker with knowledge of this public default can forge arbitrary JWTs with any role including superadmin.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"If JWT_SECRET env var is unset, an attacker can forge a JWT signed with 'hireflow2024api' claiming role: 'superadmin' and arbitrary user ID, gaining unrestricted administrative access to all API endpoints including user role management and audit logs.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"token_replay\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"Medium\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Live JWT signature verification with 'hireflow2024api' did not match observed token, suggesting JWT_SECRET env var may be set at container runtime via mechanism not visible on disk. However code-level fallback is confirmed. Test: forge HS256 JWT with secret 'hireflow2024api' and { id: valid_user_id, role: 'superadmin', exp: now+86400 } against GET /api/admin/dashboard.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"ID\\\": \\\"AUTH-VULN-13\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerability_type\\\": \\\"Login_Flow_Logic\\\",\\n      \\\"externally_exploitable\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_endpoint\\\": \\\"POST /api/auth/login\\\",\\n      \\\"vulnerable_code_location\\\": \\\"src/auth/auth.controller.js:94-96\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_defense\\\": \\\"After successful password verification, login sets req.session.userId without calling req.session.regenerate(). If a session already exists, the same session ID is elevated to an authenticated session. saveUninitialized: false reduces practical risk but the missing regenerate() call is a structural flaw.\\\",\\n      \\\"exploitation_hypothesis\\\": \\\"An attacker who pre-plants a known session ID in the victim browser (via network interception on HTTP) and then the victim logs in, the attacker retains access to the now-authenticated session using the pre-planted session ID without knowing the victim credentials.\\\",\\n      \\\"suggested_exploit_technique\\\": \\\"session_fixation\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"Low\\\",\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Practical risk reduced by saveUninitialized: false meaning no session is issued before login under normal flow. However if any middleware initializes the session before login the fixation becomes exploitable. Low confidence for exploitation but code fix is trivial: add req.session.regenerate() before setting userId.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n\"},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:08:46.089Z"}}
